<description>
This changes 2 things in `AddrManImpl::AddSingle()`

1) The rule not to update nTime for 1h come from a time when an update meant a database update.
Now that AddrMan lives in memory it is no longer necessary, and removing it simplifies the logic.
Also on master, an announcement without a penalty (i.e. a direct self-announcement) that was more than 1h newer could not increase multiplicity; now it can.

2) There was an existing check in `AddSingle()` that prevents us from increasing the `RefCount` of an address when the same announcement is received again from a different peer, which regularly happens with gossip relay - an addr can take different routes through the network to us.
However, the check was ineffective: when the entry was first added, its `nTime` was stored with a [2h time penalty](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/9d4325f40316d2c81d34418dc075b235173d0cec/src/net_processing.cpp#L6044) applied, so `addr.nTime > pinfo->nTime` holds even for an identical announcement. As a result, a single announcement relayed to us by several peers could increase an address's multiplicity in the new table, up to 8.
Fix this by applying the penalty in the comparison too.
This is just a small logic bugfix, it doesn't change things in adversarial scenarios, since peers can just do multiple announcements with changed `nTime` if they want to boost their `RefCount` artificially.
</description>

<commits>
9d3fd4c92a addrman: Remove 1h rule for updating nTime

The rule is very old, from a time when addrman did
not live in memory but in a database.
Now, there is no real reason anymore for not updating the time
whenever new information is available.
Fingerprinting is also not an issue here:
The only way in which we leak the timestamp of an address
to the outside is via GETADDR responses, which are being cached
for about a day. This means that individual updates cannot be
observed in real time.

Also move the update below the check for new information.
Previously, an announcement without a time penalty that
triggered the nTime update would then always fail the check,
so it could not increase the multiplicity. Now, any newer
announcement is treated the same. The next commit also relies
on the check comparing against the previously stored timestamp.
Since nTime can now only increase, clamping it at 0 is no
longer necessary.

047b325c69 addrman: apply time penalty in nTime check

We can receive a single self-announcement of a node from
multiple peers if it took different paths with gossip relay.
In this case, we don't want to increase the multiplicity
of the address.
Therefore, we need to take into account the time penalty
in the check.

With this, an announcement for an address in the new table
either updates nTime and is eligible for a multiplicity increase,
or does neither.
</commits>

